    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2496.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 719,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining characteristic of our planet and the fundamental requirement for all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Phases of Water)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** What we commonly drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, and steam.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Unique (Chemical Superpowers)\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a delivery system, carrying vital minerals, nutrients, and chemicals through our bodies, through the soil, and through the oceans.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different. When water freezes into ice, its molecules expand, making it *less* dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules love to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing water striders to walk on a pond) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite how much water covers the earth, about **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans. Only 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked up in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth’s water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nBiology considers water to be the cradle of life. \n*   **Cellular Level:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. Our brains are about 73% water, and our lungs about 83%. \n*   **Metabolism:** Without water, the chemical reactions that keep living things alive (digestion, respiration, cell growth) cannot take place. \n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the landscape of our planet, drives our weather and climate, and flows through the veins of every living organism. Without it, life as we know it would simply not exist."
